          "myWords": "A basic Korean meal is a bowl of rice or noodles, a soup, and simple side dishes.\n\nMy favorite: kimchi. Food or medicine?\n\nKimchi is the king of the banchan, the side dishes, and one of the three essential elements of the basic Korean meal along with rice and soup. It is the most common side dish, and in very simple meals it may be the only one. Kimchi is a true emblem of Korean national heritage, with an entire museum devoted to it in Seoul. It is a cold, fermented, usually vegetable-based dish. The ingredients change depending on where it is made, but the crucial part is that it is preserved for a long time. It is traditionally fermented and stored in dok, the earthen jars made especially for that purpose.\n\nFermentation provides the lactic acid that is otherwise absent from Korean cuisine, which does not traditionally include dairy; the chili pepper delivers ten times more vitamin C than an orange; the fiber promotes regularity; and the spices stimulate metabolism. Combined, the ingredients are believed to protect against certain cancers and drain the arteries, as well as helping manage hypertension and diabetes.",
